<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Mid-run failover from the active access leg to the standby one, driven by
    INET's ScenarioManager (see [Config UplinkStandbyLeg] in
    omnetpp_uplink.ini).

    WHY THE FAILOVER IS DONE WITH LINK STATE AND NOT WITH ROUTING

    Which leg carries the traffic is normally decided by tsnDeviceB's IPv4
    route: its next hop is the active UE's address, resolved to that UE's MAC.
    Neither is reachable at runtime. Routes are built once by
    Ipv4NetworkConfigurator, ScenarioManager has no route command
    (ScenarioManager.cc:99-114 is the complete list), and INET does no next-hop
    liveness checking, so simply breaking a leg would stop the traffic rather
    than move it.

    So the config makes the two legs indistinguishable to everything upstream
    of the DS-TT: both UEs forward IP and both carry the same MAC address, the
    switch has no static forwarding entry and therefore floods, and the two
    legs differ only in the state of their UE to DS-TT link. That is MAC
    takeover, the same trick hot-standby routers use, and it puts the whole
    decision into channel state, which <set-channel-param> does reach.

    The gateway address in tsnDeviceB's route stops mattering under that
    arrangement: it was only ever an ARP key, and the surviving UE forwards on
    the packet's destination (tsnDeviceA), not on whose gateway address the
    frame was aimed at.

    WHY `per` AND NOT `disabled`

    A link that starts out disabled can never be brought back up. INET's
    Ethernet MAC subscribes to its transmission channel's parameter changes
    only on the branch it takes when the link came up connected
    (EthernetMacBase.cc:424), so a MAC that initialized on a disabled channel
    is never notified that the channel was re-enabled and stays `connected =
    false` for the rest of the run. Setting `disabled` the other way round does
    work, which makes the failure look like a one-directional oddity: leg 0
    goes down on cue and leg 1 never comes up.

    `per = 1` avoids that entirely. The link stays physically up, carrier and
    all, and every frame crossing it is marked errored and dropped by the
    receiving MAC (EthernetMacPhy.cc:297). Same observable effect, and the
    parameter is re-read on every packet.
-->
<scenario>
    <!-- Make before break: the standby leg starts passing traffic first, then
         the active one stops. Both land at the same simulation time, so the
         ordering only decides the fate of the frame in flight. -->
    <at t="2s">
        <set-channel-param src-module="ue[1]" src-gate="ethg" par="per" value="0"/>
        <set-channel-param src-module="ue[0]" src-gate="ethg" par="per" value="1"/>
    </at>
</scenario>
